{
 "series": "METR 50% task-completion time horizon, Time Horizon 1.1 suite",
 "unit": "minutes of human-expert task time (task length at which the agent is predicted to succeed 50% of the time)",
 "description": "Per-model 50% time horizon from METR, by model release date, 2019 to the last model METR published (Claude Mythos Preview early version, released 2026-04-07; added to METR's page 2026-05-08). Each point: METR's logistic-regression estimate with its 95% bootstrap CI. \"frontier\" is METR's own is_sota flag. Extra series: the 80% horizon on the same suite, and the original Time Horizon 1.0 suite (2019 to Nov 2025) for comparison. Values are copied unmodified from METR's published YAML files.",
 "license": "METR data and analysis code are public (github.com/METR/eval-analysis-public; metr.org/time-horizons). The repository README says \"See LICENSE file for details\" but the repository contains no LICENSE file as of 2026-10-19, so no explicit open license is stated; cite METR. Epoch AI republishes the same numbers under CC BY 4.0 (metr_time_horizons_external.csv in epoch.ai/data/benchmark_data.zip).",
 "retrieved": "2026-10-19",
 "sources": [
  "https://metr.org/assets/benchmark_results_1_1.yaml",
  "https://metr.org/assets/benchmark_results_1_0.yaml",
  "https://metr.org/time-horizons/",
  "https://metr.org/blog/2026-01-29-time-horizon-1-1/",
  "https://metr.org/blog/2025-03-19-measuring-ai-ability-to-complete-long-tasks/",
  "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14499",
  "https://github.com/METR/eval-analysis-public",
  "https://epoch.ai/data/benchmark_data.zip (metr_time_horizons_external.csv, cross-check)"
 ],
 "doubling_time": {
  "metr_headline": "about 7 months, 2019 to early 2025 (METR, Mar 19, 2025 paper and post)",
  "th1_1_from_2023_on_days": {
   "ci_high": 158.012,
   "ci_low": 104.428,
   "point_estimate": 128.744
  },
  "th1_1_all_time_stitched_days": {
   "point_estimate": 187.778
  },
  "th1_0_all_time_days": {
   "ci_high": 225.353,
   "ci_low": 176.208,
   "point_estimate": 201.15
  },
  "th1_0_from_2023_on_days": {
   "ci_high": 229.176,
   "ci_low": 138.411,
   "point_estimate": 175.603
  },
  "th1_1_since_2024_days_as_of_jan_2026": 88.6,
  "notes": "TH1.1 values are from benchmark_results_1_1.yaml as of the page's last update (Sept 8, 2026); the file states the fit \"excludes points with central estimate p50 > 16 hrs\". 128.7 days is about 4.2 months; 187.8 days about 6.2 months; 201.2 days about 6.6 months. The since-2024 figure (88.6 days, about 2.9 months) is from METR's Jan 29, 2026 TH1.1 post, before METR's Mar 3, 2026 regularization correction, and is not in the YAML."
 },
 "caveats": [
  "All tasks are automatically scored: METR's suite (RE-Bench, HCAST, short software tasks) is software engineering, ML and cybersecurity tasks \"designed to be self-contained and well-specified, with clear success criteria that can be automatically evaluated.\" METR's Aug 2025 follow-up found agent performance drops substantially when work is scored holistically rather than algorithmically. This is a verifiable-domain series.",
  "The top end is a lower bound. METR: \"Measurements above 16 hrs are unreliable with our current task suite.\" Only 31 of 228 TH1.1 tasks take humans 8 hours or more, and only 5 of those have measured human baselines. The Claude Mythos Preview point (17.4 h, 95% CI 8.5 to 55 h) is labeled by METR \"likely at least 16 hrs\".",
  "METR stopped updating: its page says \"This page is no longer actively updated\" (last updated Sept 8, 2026). No model released after April 2026 has a METR horizon. Coverage was never complete: METR says its reporting \"shouldn't\" be treated as a complete record of the most capable models.",
  "Human task times come from low-context contractors (about 5 years of experience) and METR says they likely overestimate how long an in-context professional takes.",
  "The 50% horizon is a property of the logistic fit, not wall-clock agent time; agents are typically several times faster than humans on tasks they complete.",
  "TH1.1 changed the task suite (73 tasks added, 15 removed, 53 updated) and moved infrastructure to Inspect; TH1.0 and TH1.1 values for the same model differ (e.g. GPT-5: 131 vs 203 min). Do not mix the two suites in one line except as METR does for pre-2023 models.",
  "METR fits an exponential and says a logistic fit is poor \"because we haven't seen any evidence of the exponential growth in time horizon slowing down,\" and that fitting only the left side of a logistic gives \"wildly different asymptotes\" from small data changes. Any logistic projection drawn from these points is the author's model, not METR's.",
  "The two highest points are not ordinary releases: Claude Opus 4.6 (Feb 2026) has an upper CI of about 61 hours, and Claude Mythos Preview was an early version of a model Anthropic chose not to release generally. Coverage after Mar 2026 is a single model.",
  "Confidence intervals are wide (typically 2x to 5x at the top); the ratio_vs_previous_frontier field compares point estimates only."
 ],
